::  name       comlink link frame nesting bound
::  shape      bytes -- int (@u32), bound 554 -- the room a link frame leaves for a whole datagram
::  invariant  a frame this tree sends holds the datagram beneath its headers, and the compiler says so
::  example    554
::  readers    Comlink - Aurora - Mantra - Glow Tend - shape museum
::  nib        comlink-nesting-v1 at 20260830.204814
::
::  Comlink's fourth pedestal, and the third of its bounds. The first desk named the room a
::  sealed datagram leaves for its message -- 340 bytes. The second named what happens when the
::  thing carried is larger than that, and gets cut into counted pieces. This one names the
::  layer BELOW both: the link frame that has to hold a whole datagram, headers and all.
::
::  THE RELATION, IN ONE LINE. Every layer of a network stack wraps the layer above it, so each
::  container must hold its own header plus the whole payload it carries:
::
::    container >= header + payload
::
::  Break it and a fixed buffer is written past its end. There is no truncation and no refusal
::  at the door -- the bytes simply land outside the array, in a bare-metal guest, at a fixed
::  address in guest RAM.
::
::  THE TWO LAYERS, MEASURED ON METAL 20260830 by building them and printing the constants:
::
::    layer     container            header               payload              need  slack
::    datagram  wire_capacity  528   off_cipher     188   max_message    340    528      0
::    frame     max_frame      554   link_headers    26   wire_capacity  528    554      0
::
::  `link_headers` is `virtio_net_hdr_len` plus `eth_header_len` -- 12 and 14. The first is
::  `@sizeOf(VirtioNetHdr)` rather than a literal, so it is the struct's own width, and it is a
::  width only while the struct stays padding-free, which device_wire.rye proves at comptime.
::
::  BOTH STAND AT ZERO, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS SAFE. That is the whole finding, and it is worth
::  saying plainly because zero slack looks equally alarming in both rows and is not.
::
::  The datagram row is zero because it is DERIVED. comlink/wire_format.rye writes
::  `max_message = wire_capacity - off_cipher`, so the payload is defined as whatever the header
::  leaves behind. Raise the capacity and the message grows with it, in the same expression, on
::  the same line. The relation cannot come apart, because there is only one number.
::
::  The frame row is zero because it is SPELLED. `max_frame` is the literal 554 in
::  comlink/virtio_net.rye, `wire_capacity` is the literal 528 in comlink/wire_format.rye, and
::  until 20260830 the only thing joining them was a doc comment above the first:
::
::    /// Room for virtio/eth link headers plus a full `wire_format.wire_capacity` datagram
::    /// (528 + 12 + 14).
::
::  Correct to the byte, and inert. A sentence does not stop a build.
::
::  WHY THE TWO ROOMS CANNOT SIMPLY CHECK EACH OTHER. comlink/virtio_net.rye is freestanding --
::  it drives virtio-net MMIO from a riscv64 guest with no operating system, defines its own
::  three-line `assert`, and imports nothing. comlink/wire_format.rye reaches std, std.debug.print,
::  and kumara for the signature. Neither can import the other, so the arithmetic has nowhere to
::  live except a third room that sees both -- which is exactly why it ended up in prose.
::
::  MEASURED, RATHER THAN REASONED. The plausible real edit is a capacity a growing tree raises,
::  and it was pressed on metal before a line of this was written:
::
::    wire_capacity 528 -> 576, nothing else changed
::    the guest still builds:  rye build comlink/guest_2way_source_tx.rye
::                             -target riscv64-freestanding-none    GREEN
::    the same arithmetic run: index out of bounds: index 602, len 554
::
::  Nothing refused. The frame overrun is entirely a runtime fact, in a guest with no operating
::  system underneath it to notice, and the build that produced it was green.
::
::  WHO LEANS ON IT. 57 of Comlink's 59 freestanding guests import both rooms, and 27 of them lay
::  a sealed datagram into the frame with the identical four lines:
::
::    const poff = eoff + vn.eth_header_len;                 //  12 + 14 = 26
::    tally_copy.copy_disjoint(u8, buf[poff..][0..dg_len], scratch[0..dg_len]);
::    const total: u32 = poff + dg_len;                      //  26 + 528 = 554
::    tx.send(total);                                        //  asserts total <= max_frame
::
::  `dg_len` is `off_cipher + message.len`, so a short message never reaches the ceiling and a
::  full one lands on it exactly. The failure is content-dependent in the same way the bead
::  family's was: a fixture carrying a short message passes forever.
::
::  WHAT IS WIRED, AND WHAT IS ONLY WRITTEN. From 20260830 the relation is published where the
::  container lives and evaluated where both numbers are visible:
::
::    comlink/virtio_net.rye   `frame_carries(wire_capacity)` -- the arithmetic, published by the
::                             room that owns max_frame, so a reader moving that number meets it
::    comlink/device_wire.rye  two comptime asserts -- the relation called with wire_format's own
::                             capacity, and the padding-free proof the header width leans on,
::                             restated here so deleting it cannot quietly widen the header
::
::  The tie was proven able to REFUSE before it shipped -- inverted, and then by the plausible
::  real edits: `wire_capacity` raised to 576 stops the build, `eth_header_len` raised to 18 for
::  a VLAN tag stops the build, a field added to VirtioNetHdr stops the build. A tie proven only
::  in the passing direction cannot be told from a comptime block nobody evaluates.
::
::  THREE READINGS CARRY THIS DESK, and each can fail while the other two agree. The rows the
::  desk lists equal what the Rye publishes, so a bound moved in one room and not the desk is
::  seen. The relation holds for both rows by the scan's own arithmetic, computed rather than
::  pinned, so an honest raise in both rooms walks free. And the comptime tie still stands in the
::  room that holds it -- delete that line and the first two readings agree perfectly over code
::  nothing proves will carry what it sends.
::
::  THE SLACK IS REPORTED, NEVER GATED. A lap that raises `max_frame` to a full ethernet MTU
::  moves the frame row's slack from 0 to something comfortable, and that is better work rather
::  than a fault. A guard that reds on an improvement is a guard someone turns off.
::
::  WHAT THIS DESK DOES NOT REACH. Whether 554 is the right frame size, which is Comlink's and
::  Aurora's design question; this room reads it. Whether the guests SHOULD each carry the tie --
::  the relation is between constants, so one evaluation settles it for all 57, and the reading
::  is a FLOOR rather than an equality, so a guest taking its own copy walks free. And the
::  hosted UDP lane in comlink/hosted_wire.rye, which hands its datagram to the kernel and
::  declares no frame of its own: measured, and there is no third row to show.
+$  comlink-link-frame-nesting-shape
  $:  bytes=@u32
  ==
